{"name":"Great Blue Heron","hex":"#d5e0ee","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"great blue heron","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large wading bird of the species Ardea herodias."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/great_blue_heron"}],"entries":[{"title":"Great blue heron","blocks":[{"text":"The great blue heron is a large wading bird in the heron family (Ardeidae), common near shores of open water and wetlands over most of North and Central America, as well as Northwestern South America, the Caribbean, and the Galápagos Islands. It is occasionally found in the Azores and is a rare vagrant to Europe. An all-white population found in South Florida, the Florida Keys, and the Caribbean islands, is known as the great white heron. Debate exists about whether these white birds are a color morph of the great blue heron, a subspecies of it, or an entirely separate species."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/GreatBlueHeronInARiver.jpg/330px-GreatBlueHeronInARiver.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_blue_heron","wikidata":"Q333796"},{"title":"Great Blue Heron (sculpture)","blocks":[{"text":"Great Blue Heron is an outdoor 2012 sculpture by Jud Turner, installed at the intersection of East 13th Avenue and Alder Street in Eugene, Oregon, in the United States. The 16-foot (4.9 m) sculpture is made of fifty percent recycled materials and fifty percent new steel. It was unveiled on October 18, 2012, two days before Eugene's 150th birthday. The work was commissioned by The Duck Store, the University of Oregon, and the City of Eugene."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/a1/Blue-Heron-Sculpture-Eugene-Oregon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Blue_Heron_(sculpture)","wikidata":"Q21160896"},{"title":"Heron","blocks":[{"text":"Herons are long-legged, long-necked, freshwater and coastal birds in the family Ardeidae, with 75 recognised species, some of which are referred to as egrets or bitterns rather than herons. Members of the genus Botaurus are referred to as bitterns, and, together with the zigzag heron, or zigzag bittern, in the monotypic genus Zebrilus, form a monophyletic group, Botaurinae, within the Ardeidae family. Egrets do not form a biologically distinct group from herons, and tend to be named differently because they are mainly white or have decorative plumes in breeding plumage. Herons, by evolutionary adaptation, have long beaks."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Ardeidae-01.jpg/330px-Ardeidae-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heron","wikidata":"Q18789"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Great_blue_heron_%28Ardea_herodias_occidentalis%29_white_form.JPG/500px-Great_blue_heron_%28Ardea_herodias_occidentalis%29_white_form.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Great_blue_heron_%28Ardea_herodias_occidentalis%29_white_form.JPG/1280px-Great_blue_heron_%28Ardea_herodias_occidentalis%29_white_form.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Great_blue_heron_%28Ardea_herodias_occidentalis%29_white_form.JPG/500px-Great_blue_heron_%28Ardea_herodias_occidentalis%29_white_form.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGreat_blue_heron_(Ardea_herodias_occidentalis)_white_form.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Great_Blue_Heron_%289012933612%29.jpg/500px-Great_Blue_Heron_%289012933612%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Great_Blue_Heron_%289012933612%29.jpg/1280px-Great_Blue_Heron_%289012933612%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Great_Blue_Heron_%289012933612%29.jpg/500px-Great_Blue_Heron_%289012933612%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGreat_Blue_Heron_(9012933612).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Blue_Heron%2C_Lake_Sammamish_State_Park.jpg/500px-Blue_Heron%2C_Lake_Sammamish_State_Park.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Blue_Heron%2C_Lake_Sammamish_State_Park.jpg/1280px-Blue_Heron%2C_Lake_Sammamish_State_Park.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/com